# =============================================================================
# NOT REDISTRIBUTED: Canyon Code's supplied material
# =============================================================================
# This repository is public. It contains the measurement harness, the
# optimizations and the results -- all of which are my work. It deliberately
# does NOT contain the assignment's supplied workflow or its query corpus:
# publishing an employer's take-home exercise would leak it for every future
# candidate, and it is not mine to republish.
#
# To run this repo you need those files. Drop them in:
#
#   workflow/portfolio/agents/{price,metrics,risk,advisor}_agent.py
#   workflow/portfolio/workflows/portfolio_workflow.py
#   data/queries.json
#
# PATCHES.md documents every change made to them, without reproducing them.
# parser_agent.py and parser_cascade.py ARE included: both are new stages
# written for this project, not part of the handout.
workflow/portfolio/agents/price_agent.py
workflow/portfolio/agents/metrics_agent.py
workflow/portfolio/agents/risk_agent.py
workflow/portfolio/agents/advisor_agent.py
workflow/portfolio/workflows/portfolio_workflow.py
workflow/portfolio/config/
docs/upstream/
data/queries.json

# =============================================================================
# environment
# =============================================================================
.venv/
ccenv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.env
nohup.out
*.tar.gz

# =============================================================================
# logs
# =============================================================================
# vLLM server logs carry the cluster hostname, the home directory path and the
# full launch argv. None of it is secret and none of it is something a reader
# needs -- the CONFIGURATION is what matters, and that lives in
# results/*/manifest.json with the hostname stored as a truncated SHA-256.
# Excluded rather than sanitised: hand-scrubbing a 650 KB log is how a hostname
# ends up in a public repo.
logs/

# =============================================================================
# frozen price snapshot
# =============================================================================
# Provider data (yfinance/Yahoo). Redistributing it raises a licensing question
# this project has no need to answer, because MANIFEST.json carries a sha256
# for every ticker file plus the snapshot_id that every run records. Anyone can
# rebuild the snapshot and PROVE it matches the one these results came from:
#
#   python scripts/build_snapshot.py     # then compare snapshot_id
#
# Verifiability without redistribution.
data/snapshot/*.json
!data/snapshot/MANIFEST.json

# =============================================================================
# run outputs
# =============================================================================
# KEPT -- these are the evidence:
#   manifest.json      what the run claimed: config, versions, checksums
#   validity.json      every assertion and its verdict
#   validity.recheck.json  the same run re-judged under the CURRENT rules,
#                      which are stricter than the ones it was made under.
#                      Kept precisely because it is the unflattering file.
#   report.json        the computed metrics
#   parser_eval.json   parser correctness
#   advisor_eval.json  briefing quality gates
#   counters.json      LLM call counts and the Tier-1/Tier-2 split
#   gpu.json           telemetry
#   failures.jsonl     what broke and why -- small, and the most informative file
#
# EXCLUDED -- large and regenerable by re-running:
#   trace.jsonl        every span and LLM call, ~5 MB per 1000-query run
#   results.jsonl      per-query output including full briefings, ~3 MB
#                      (also contains the supplied query text verbatim)
results/**/trace.jsonl
results/**/results.jsonl
results/server.json

# failures.jsonl records the full query on every row, because that is what
# makes a failure debuggable locally. Same supplied text as results.jsonl, so
# same rule. The failure TAXONOMY still reaches the repo through report.json,
# which carries query_id, error class and the parse but not the sentence.
results/**/failures.jsonl

# =============================================================================
# the perturbed robustness set
# =============================================================================
# Not published, and not redactable either. Its control group is verbatim
# corpus text and two further groups keep Canyon Code's sentence structure with
# entities substituted -- so the phrasing is theirs even where the companies
# are not. Stripping the text would leave a file of ids that cannot be re-run,
# which is worse than not shipping it.
#
# What IS published: the composition and the scores, in
# data/query_sets/perturbed.summary.json. The robustness result stands without
# the corpus travelling with it, and
#
#   python scripts/perturbed_set.py --build
#
# rebuilds an equivalent set for anyone holding an authorised corpus.
data/query_sets/perturbed.json
data/query_sets/perturbed.local.json

